# Encrypted Prisma queries

Fields marked `/// @encrypted` (and companion `*Hash` columns) cannot use string filters that compare ciphertext.

## Never on encrypted fields

- `in` / `notIn` / `contains` / `startsWith` / `endsWith` / `mode: 'insensitive'`
- `updateMany({ where: { plaidId: accountId } })` — ciphertext will not match plaintext

Examples: `AccountRegister.plaidId`, `plaidAccessToken`, `RegisterEntry.plaidId`, `User.email`, names/descriptions that are encrypted.

## Allowed

- Equality (`where: { plaidAccessToken: token }`) — extension rewrites to the hash column when configured.
- Null checks (`plaidId: { not: null }`).
- Filter by **non-encrypted** keys (`id`, `accountId`, etc.).

## Pattern: load then filter in memory

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
await db.accountRegister.findMany({
  where: { plaidId: { in: plaidAccountIds } },
});

// ✅ GOOD — equality on token, then filter decrypted plaidId
const rows = await db.accountRegister.findMany({
  where: { plaidAccessToken: accessToken, plaidId: { not: null } },
});
const wanted = new Set(plaidAccountIds);
return rows.filter((r) => r.plaidId != null && wanted.has(r.plaidId));
```

Updates: `where: { id: registerId }`, not encrypted field equality for bulk writes when unsure.

Details: `.agent/logic/encryption-and-keys.mdc`. Reference: `PlaidSyncService.findPlaidRegistersByAccessToken`.
